        "School, Boundary and Neighborhood Cluster Statistics File The School, Boundary and Neighborhood Cluster Statistics file provides information about the characteristics of the DCPS elementary schools of right, information about students living in the current school of right boundaries, and future population projections. For example, if you look at Aiton Elementary School in the other data tab, the first couple of columns [columns B thru J] provides characteristics of the school such as its enrollment, capacity, the percent of in-boundary students, and the percent of at risk students ENROLLED at the school (the denominator in these cases is the total number of students enrolled at the school). Data in columns K-P describes boundary-level information, such as the percent of students living in the boundary and attending their assigned school, as well as the percent of students whom are identified as at risk and attending their assigned school (the denominator in these cases is all grade-appropriate public school students LIVING in the boundary). In an effort to allow the public to compare the number of students subtracted and added to the proposed boundaries released to the public in April, the file also provides the number of public school students that live in the current elementary school boundaries versus the number in the proposed boundaries. Please note, the proposed boundaries released in April are not final. Another version of the proposed boundaries will be released to the public in June, and will be revised based on community feedback and additional analysis . The last couple of columns in the data file [Q thru R] provides neighborhood cluster information on the future growth or decline in child population projected for the neighborhood cluster where the school is located. See the data definition tab included in the spreadsheet for a more complete description of the data."
